          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong double profunctor
            is an appropriate notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
            between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
          %p
            Thinking of a profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H : C &amp;#8696; D
            as representing some notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heteromorphism"} heteromorphism
            %em vertical
            heteromorphism or
            %em horizontal
            heteromorphism. For clarity, this article focuses on the horizontal double profunctors. Vertical double profunctors can be defined by taking the transpose.
          %p
            That is,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            defines for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C, d \in D
            a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(d,c)
            of horizontal heteromorphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in H(d,c)
            can be composed with horizontal morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : d' \to d
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : c \to c'
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to yield a heteromorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ;
                    %mi h
                    %mo ;
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f;h;g) \in H(d',c')
          %p
            Furthermore,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            defines, for each heteromorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi h
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in H(d,c), h' \in H(d',c')
            and
            %em vertical
            arrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : d \to d'
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta : c \to c'
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi d
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi h
                        %mtd
                          %mi c
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ⇓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi ϕ
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi β
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi d
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi h
                              %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mi c
                          %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{matrix}
                  d
                  \&amp;\stackrel{h}{\to} &amp;
                  c
                  \\\
                  {}^{\mathllap{\alpha}}\downarrow
                  \&amp;\Downarrow^{\mathrlap{\phi}}&amp;
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\beta}}
                  \\\
                  d'
                  \&amp;\underset{h'}{\to} &amp;
                  c'
                  \\end{matrix}
          %p
            These can be composed vertically with hetero-2-cells along
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h,h'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D, C
            of the appropriate shape along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ,
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha, \beta
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p A double profunctor can be defined in several equivalent ways. In each case, however, we must choose whether to regard the “vertical” or the “horizontal” direction as primary; applying transposes results in a different notion of double profunctor.
          %h3#as_internal_profunctors_in_cat As internal profunctors in Cat
          %p
            Since a (strict) double category is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %strong double profunctor
            to be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+profunctor"} internal profunctor
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are double categories, a double profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C &amp;#8696; D
            consists of a span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ←
                  %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0 \leftarrow H \to D_0
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                  %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \times_{C_0} H \to H
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\times_{D_0} D_1 \to H
            which are associative and commute with each other.
          %h3#as_collages_in_dblcat As collages in DblCat
          %p
            Another natural way to define a double profunctor is in terms of its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/collage"} collage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C &amp;#8696; D
            consists of a double category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to H
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\to H
            such that:
          %ol
            %li
              The images of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              are disjoint.
            %li
              The induced functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\sqcup D\to H
              is bijective on objects and vertical arrows.
            %li
              Each functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to H
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\to H
              is fully faithful on horizontal arrows and squares.
            %li
              There are no horizontal arrows in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              from an object of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              to an object of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and whose horizontal target is a vertical arrow in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            In order to identify this with the previous definition as written, for a double category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = C_1 \;\rightrightarrows\; C_0
            written as an internal category in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
            vertically and the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1
            horizontally.
          %p
            Defined in this way, double profunctors can be seen to be precisely the two-sided
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+cofibration"} codiscrete cofibrations
            in the 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DblCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblCat
            of double categories, double functors, and horizontal transformations.
          %h3#as_spanvalued_double_functors As Span-valued double functors
          %p
            A third natural way to define double profunctors is as functors defined on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{op}\times C
            %em lax
            double functors from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{op}\times C
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Span
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{op}
            denotes horizontal reversal, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Span
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span
            denotes the (pseudo) double category whose objects are sets, whose horizontal arrows are functions, and whose vertical arrows are spans.
          %h3#explicit_definition Explicit definition
          %p
            Each of these definitions can be unraveled to give a more explicit definition, and thereby shown to be the same. Explicitly, a double profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\colon C &amp;#8696; D
            consists of the following.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn 0
                        %mi ⇸
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^0\colon C^0 &amp;#8696; D^0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^0
                denotes the
                %em horizontal
                category of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                An ordinary profunctor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                        %mi ⇸
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1 \colon C^1  &amp;#8696; D^1
            %li
              %p
                Each
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi q
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in H^1(q,p)
                is assigned a vertical source
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(r) \in H^0(s(q),s(p))
                and target
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi r
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(r) \in H^0(t(q),t(p))
            %li
              %p
                Each
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in H^0(d,c)
                is assigned an identity
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mi h
                        %mo ∈
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mi d
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_h \in H^1(1_d,1_c)
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_1 \in H^1(q_1,p_1)
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_2 \in H^1(q_2,p_2)
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(r_1) = s(r_2)
                (hence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(q_1)=s(q_2)
                and
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(p_1)=s(p_2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ⊟
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_2 \boxminus r_1 \in H^1(q_2 q_1,p_2 p_1)
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ⊟
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(r_2\boxminus r_1) = s(r_1)
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi r
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ⊟
                        %msub
                          %mi r
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi r
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(r_2\boxminus r_1) = t(r_2)
            %li
              %p
                The composition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \boxminus
                is associative and unital (with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi h
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_h
                as identities), and interchanges with the action of the profunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1
                and the vertical composition of squares in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                As with any notion of profunctor, the appropriate
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom+functor"} hom
                should be a double profunctor. In fact, since there are 2 kinds of morphism, there are 2 different hom profunctors. Following the convention above, the
                %em horizontal
                hom is a profunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mi ⇸
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C &amp;#8696; C
                and is given by the canonical span
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi s
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi t
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0 \stackrel{s}{\leftarrow} C_1 \stackrel{t}{\to} C_0
                and the vertical hom is just the horizontal hom on the transpose.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-categories"} strict 2-categories
                regarded as vertically-discrete double categories, then a double profunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mi ⇸
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C &amp;#8696; D
                is the same as a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mi op
                        %mo ×
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{op}\times C \to Cat
            %li
              %p
                On the other hand, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                are 2-categories regarded as
                = succeed "-discrete" do
                  %em horizontally
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mi ⇸
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C &amp;#8696; D
                is the same as a lax 2-functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mi co
                        %mo ×
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi Span
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{co}\times C \to Span
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mi co
                        %mo ×
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi Prof
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{co}\times C \to Prof
          %h2#weakenings Weakenings
          %p
            The definition can be weakened to the case when the composition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is weak in one direction or the other, or both (modulo a suitable definition of the latter situation, see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            for several possibilities).
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The internal definition can easily be weakened in the case when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                are
                %em horizontally
                pseudo, since then they are simply pseudo internal categories in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %li
              %p
                The collage definition, on the other hand, can easily be weakened to the case when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                are
                %em vertically
                pseudo, by looking at codiscrete cofibrations in the 2-category of vertically-pseudo double categories, pseudo double functors, and horizontal transformations. The explicit description of the collages could also be given for horizontally or doubly pseudo double categories, although in those cases it would be harder to identify them with codiscrete cofibrations, since horizontal transformations would no longer be the 2-cells in a 2-category (only a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
                of some sort).
            %li
              %p
                The Span-valued double functor definition makes perfect sense as written if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                are vertically pseudo, and also if they are horizontally or doubly pseudo, modulo a suitable definition of “vertically lax functor” in the latter two cases.
            %li
              %p
                The explicit definition can also easily be weakened, with an insertion of coherence isomorphisms for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                in appropriate places.
          %p
            There is also a natural notion of profunctor between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+categories"} virtual double categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"} virtual equipment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi vDblProf
                  %mo =
                  %mi KMod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Span
                  %mo ,
                  %mi fc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} vDblProf = KMod(Span,fc)
            whose objects are virtual double categories (here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi fc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fc
            is the free-category monad on
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Span
                    %mo =
                    %mi Span
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span = Span(Set)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are (possibly vertically pseudo) double categories, regarded as vertically-virtual double categories, then any double profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mi ⇸
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C &amp;#8696; D
            in the sense of the above definitions can also be considered as a “virtual-double profunctor” in a straightforward way. However, not every virtual-double profunctor between double categories is a double profunctor; those that are are those satisfying a “representability” property.
          %h2#composites Composites
          %p
            Composition of double profunctors is, unfortunately, hard to define and not well-behaved. Several of the above definitions suggest a possible way to compose them. However, not all of these ideas are well-defined, and those that are well-defined are not associative. In particular, there is not a double category of double categories, double functors, and double profunctors (although there are various weaker structures, such as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+category"} virtual double category
            and a
            = succeed ")." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                lax double category
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/lax+double+category"} ?
          %h3#using_internal_profunctors Using internal profunctors
          %p
            Internal profunctors can be composed, using a coequalizer, in any category which has coequalizers preserved by pullback. However, while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            has coequalizers, they are not preserved by pullback, so this does not work – it is not even possible to define actions making the putative “composite” into a double profunctor. (This failure has nothing to do with strictness or weakness;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-colimits"} 2-colimits
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            are also not preserved by
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-pullbacks"} 2-pullbacks
          %p
            This definition does, however, suggest a replacement for the nonexistent double category of double profunctors. Namely, internal categories, functors, and profunctors in
            %em any
            category with pullbacks always form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+category"} virtual double category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"} virtual equipment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DblProf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblProf
            of double categories, double functors, and double profunctors. In terms of the explicit definition, a cell in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DblProf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblProf
            with some given boundary consists of:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An ordinary cell in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Prof
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^0
            %li
              %p
                Another ordinary cell in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Prof
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^1
            %li
              %p Vertical sources, targets, identities, and composites are respected.
          %p
            For many purposes, having a virtual equipment of double profunctors is sufficient. For instance, this is all we need in order to talk about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limits"} weighted limits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategories"} generalized multicategories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
          %p
            The virtual equipment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DblProf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblProf
            is also contained as a non-full sub-virtual-equipment of the virtual equipment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi vDblProf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} vDblProf
            of virtual double categories, functors, and virtual-double profunctors mentioned above.
          %h3#using_collages Using collages
          %p
            The standard way to compose codiscrete cofibrations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                  %mo ←
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to H \leftarrow D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mo ←
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\to K\leftarrow E
            is to take the pushout (or 2-pushout)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mi D
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\sqcup_D K
            and then factor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊔
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mi D
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\sqcup E \to H\sqcup_D K
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊔
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mi D
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\sqcup E \to H\circ K \to H\sqcup_D K
            in some way such as to make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊔
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\sqcup E \to H\circ K
            codiscrete. We can do this for codiscrete cofibrations in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DblCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblCat
            and thereby obtain composites of double profunctors. However, the resulting composition operation is not associative, since pushouts in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DblCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblCat
            do not preserve the requisite factorizations.
          %p
            I believe that the binary composite of double profunctors defined in this way is a “weak composite” in the virtual double category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DblProf
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblProf
            = succeed "-ary" do
